#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175757 :question/id #uuid "69c10628-c8f2-58c6-acea-13356540156b" :question/math-academy-id "e-21814" :question/problem "The rate at which the balance in a savings account grows $B(t),$ after $t$ years, can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d B}{\\textrm d t} = 0.05B.\n$$\n\nHow many years does it take for the balance to increase by $60\\%?$  Round your answer to the nearest year." :question/worked-solution "We model the balance growth using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d B}{\\textrm d t} = 0.05B.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nB(t) = B_0\\,e^{0.05t},\n$$\n\nwhere $B_0$ is the initial balance.\n\nLet $t_1$ be the time when the balance has increased by $60\\%.$ That is,\n\n$$\nB(t_1) = 1.6B_0.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for $t_1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B({t}_{1}) & ={B}_{0}{e}^{0.05\\cdot {t}_{1}} \\\\ 1.6{B}_{0} & ={B}_{0}{e}^{0.05{t}_{1}} \\\\ 1.6 & ={e}^{0.05{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.6) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{0.05{t}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.6) & =0.05{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.6)}{0.05} \\\\ {t}_{1} & ≈9.40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest integer, we conclude that it takes $9$ years for the balance to increase by $60\\%.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175758 :question/id #uuid "670e9897-4cbd-500b-ab4b-73441c16e4a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-21815" :question/problem "The rate at which the amount of medicine in a person’s bloodstream decreases $M(t),$ after $t$ hours, can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}M}{\\textrm{d}t} = -0.08M.\n$$\n\nHow many hours does it take for the amount of medicine to decrease to $30\\%$ of the original amount?  Round your answer to the nearest hour." :question/worked-solution "We model the amount of medicine using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}M}{\\textrm{d}t} = -0.08M.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t) = M_0\\,e^{-0.08t},\n$$\n\nwhere $M_0$ is the initial amount of medicine.\n\nLet $t_1$ be the time when the amount of medicine is $30\\%$ of the original amount. That is,\n\n$$\nM(t_1) = 0.30M_0.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for $t_1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.30{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.08{t}_{1}} \\\\ 0.30 & ={e}^{-0.08{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.30) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-0.08{t}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.30) & =-0.08{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.30)}{0.08} \\\\ {t}_{1} & ≈15.050\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest integer, we conclude that it takes $15$ hours for the amount of medicine to decrease to $30\\%$ of the original amount."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175759 :question/id #uuid "37b9686a-dec8-57d5-acda-9655da7de049" :question/math-academy-id "e-21816" :question/problem "During an experiment, the weight $W(t)$ of an object varies according to the differential equation $W'(t) = -r W(t),$ where $t$ is the time, measured in hours, and $r > 0.$ It is known that the object's weight reduces by three-quarters after the first two hours. How long does it take for the object's weight to reduce to one-sixth of its original weight? Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nW(t) = W_0 e^{-rt},\n$$\n\nwhere $W_0$ is the weight when $t=0.$\n\nLet $t_1$ be the time when the weight equals $\\dfrac{1}{6}W_0.$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({t}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{6}{W}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{W}_{0}{e}^{-r{t}_{1}}=\\frac{1}{6}{W}_{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for $t_1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{0}{e}^{-r{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{6}{W}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{W}_{0}}{e}^{-r{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cancel{{W}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{-r{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-r{t}_{1}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right) \\\\ -r{t}_{1} & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡6 \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}{r}\\,\\,(∗)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate $r.$ We're told that the weight reduces by three-quarters after the first two hours. Therefore, one-quarter of the weight remains, and we have\n\n$$\nW(2) = \\dfrac{1}{4}W_0\\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad W_0e^{-2r} = \\dfrac{1}{4}W_0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for $r,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{0}{e}^{-2r} & =\\frac{1}{4}{W}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{W}_{0}}{e}^{-2r} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cancel{{W}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{-2r} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-2r}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right) \\\\ -2r & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ r & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡{4}^{1/2} \\\\ r & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this into $(*)$ and evaluating, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}{r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & ≈2.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place. \n\nTherefore, we conclude that it takes approximately $2.6$ hours for the weight to reduce to one-sixth of its original weight."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175760 :question/id #uuid "0936cc2b-c330-5f40-b87c-538cb60ccda4" :question/math-academy-id "e-21817" :question/problem "A marathon runner is participating in a race. The remaining distance $D(t)$ they have left to run $t$ hours since starting the race can be modeled by the differential equation $D'(t)= -0.8D(t).$ Two hours into the race, they have $6\\,\\text{km}$ remaining. Calculate the distance remaining after four hours." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nD(t) = D_0\\,e^{-0.8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $D_0$ is the remaining distance when $t=0.$\n\nWe are given that the remaining distance was $6\\,\\text{km}$ after $2$ hours. So, substituting $t=2$ and $D(2)=6$ into our general solution and solving for $D_0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(2) & ={D}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.8\\cdot 2} \\\\ 6 & ={D}_{0}\\,{e}^{-1.6} \\\\ {D}_{0} & =\\frac{6}{{e}^{-1.6}} \\\\  & ≈29.718,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nHence, our particular solution is\n\n$$\nD(t) = 29.718\\,e^{-0.8t}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the distance remaining after $4$ hours. So, substituting $t=4$ into our particular solution, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(4) & =29.718\\,{e}^{-0.8\\cdot 4} \\\\  & =29.718\\,{e}^{-3.2} \\\\  & ≈1.2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, the remaining distance after $4$ hours is $1.2\\,\\text{km}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176466 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "881c470b-a83e-5a02-9912-d91e06452ba4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$15.6$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174935} {:answer/id #uuid "b1bf2195-70d8-574e-9bf8-9ade67657af5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$15.1$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174936} {:answer/id #uuid "cfdefe0f-c244-5f1a-9427-72546234421d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$15.4$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174937} {:answer/id #uuid "fda8aa62-fb36-5850-aaa3-19c16f724d6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$15.8$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174938} {:answer/id #uuid "7a7ac70b-dae0-5c30-92ee-6740c80f77e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$15.3$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174939}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b28aaca2-dd9b-573e-8c4c-a5d3119f76ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174934}] :question/id #uuid "a0731b51-114f-5c62-b117-cf575f2bb712" :question/math-academy-id "q-339217" :question/problem "The price $P(t)$ of a particular good varies according to the differential equation $P^{′}(t) = rP(t)$, where $t$ is the time, measured in years, and $r > 0$. It is known that the price of the good doubled after the first ten years. How long did it take for the price to triple from the initial price? Round your answer to one decimal place.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176467 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81232edc-4c8e-5351-bb77-6f3401ea092f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.4$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174941} {:answer/id #uuid "b9510935-6326-5106-86c9-d73cfc4d660c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174942} {:answer/id #uuid "d1a79465-548b-5b86-951c-19f5a6b2baa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.2$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174943} {:answer/id #uuid "f20211b9-ecfc-56af-99b7-450761261a81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.3$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174944} {:answer/id #uuid "196311b0-6871-57ea-bb85-9aef60d82f38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.5$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174945}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d40397b4-108f-595d-bdfe-10e02feae935" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174940}] :question/id #uuid "9c839944-7243-5474-8d5d-7dae9726872c" :question/math-academy-id "q-339225" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t)$ of a particular liquid varies according to the differential equation $T^{′}(t) =-rT(t)$, where $t$ is the time, measured in minutes, and $r > 0$. It is known that the temperature reduces by four-fifths of its initial temperature after the first two minutes. How long does it take for the temperature to reduce to one-fifteenth of its original temperature? Round your answer to one decimal place.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176468 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81585735-8c65-5844-a119-4f8a1f07e813" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2241" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174947} {:answer/id #uuid "ce932d89-df43-5481-8ff1-c8e3bf36f428" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1524" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174948} {:answer/id #uuid "1319c4ad-bb90-5131-b714-fefc4ed1e7f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174949} {:answer/id #uuid "2e9c15a7-0477-5485-86b6-60014fee5462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1521" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174950} {:answer/id #uuid "5c65bccc-888f-53cf-9d14-6d14c4170c41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2145" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174951}] :answer-field/id #uuid "99d3c3c7-e145-5034-860c-efa334d6c7d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174946}] :question/id #uuid "dfc23a34-2497-5086-81f5-369d2b5abcea" :question/math-academy-id "q-339241" :question/problem "The population $P(t)$ of penguins on an island after $t$ years can be modeled by the differential equation $P^{′}(t) =-0.1P(t)$. If there were $3200$ penguins remaining on the island after $5$ years, calculate the number of penguins remaining after $9$ years.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176469 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0997007-a396-507f-88d0-5e7aa0107b05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1583" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174953} {:answer/id #uuid "d0734bcb-c851-5aad-8e71-8f9030e5ccf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3247" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174954} {:answer/id #uuid "fb1ab1b8-a238-5798-99f4-3ea81cf8bcc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2494" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174955} {:answer/id #uuid "4fbbc8aa-ff9e-5860-85ab-20aa53b50af9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2021" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174956} {:answer/id #uuid "667667c6-cf60-51b4-b389-07f387b4b8b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1732" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174957}] :answer-field/id #uuid "faa33131-0e4d-5afa-8bb0-c1a6d22d4c2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174952}] :question/id #uuid "caf47119-3642-5b13-b14f-5022ef6a4a55" :question/math-academy-id "q-339244" :question/problem "The rate $P^{′}(t)$ of a fish population varies according to the differential equation $P^{′}(t) = 0.09P(t)$, where $t$ is the time, measured in weeks. After $18$ weeks, the fish population was $8000$. What was the initial fish population?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176470 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa118334-a392-5036-8155-e1e6fc980b54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174959} {:answer/id #uuid "de396b01-7c24-5a49-a699-21d5c1a08d1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174960} {:answer/id #uuid "27fdd921-a055-5967-ad74-9257839130d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174961} {:answer/id #uuid "4ed689e6-c443-5a0f-81d6-20c1de85080e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174962} {:answer/id #uuid "6805fe67-0c4a-54f5-9bd8-317235765c87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174963}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c5757de4-3b20-5538-9863-b7af8b7ee75d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174958}] :question/id #uuid "4f4787ae-b523-5cf7-a19b-de4ae9d2710e" :question/math-academy-id "q-339269" :question/problem "The rate at which the population of a particular city grows $P(t)$, after $t$ years, can be modeled by the differential equation\n$\\frac{dP}{dt} = 0.055P$.\n\nIf the population today is $150000$, how many years will it take for the population size to increase to $400000$? Round your answer to the nearest year.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176471 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f0d9fb3-93fb-589d-834b-84e06fb9ec16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$70$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312083} {:answer/id #uuid "78bcb0ce-6db7-5ab5-860d-ac02a09bd184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$35$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312936} {:answer/id #uuid "a717f2ab-64a8-5984-bca5-e77295b80992" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313285} {:answer/id #uuid "eacd7039-bb99-5d39-ba09-4db34ae42543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$26$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313843} {:answer/id #uuid "fcf8f46a-3a14-5f05-8ecc-3709734112d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$47$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78bcb0ce-6db7-5ab5-860d-ac02a09bd184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$35$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312936} :answer-field/id #uuid "29728c60-4306-56a5-81d3-33ee8c69915b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "26f43bea-64fd-52c6-be2f-1c448fb8e680" :question/math-academy-id "q-339289" :question/problem "The rate at which the population of a particular country grows $P(t),$ after $t$ years, can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=0.02P.\n$$\n\nHow many years does it take for the population to double? Round your answer to the nearest year." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We model the population growth using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=0.02P.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.02t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{0}$ is the initial population.\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the population of the country is double the initial number. That is,\n\n$$\nP({t}_{1})=2{P}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({t}_{1}) & ={P}_{0}{e}^{0.02\\cdot {t}_{1}} \\\\ 2{P}_{0} & ={P}_{0}{e}^{0.02{t}_{1}} \\\\ 2 & ={e}^{0.02{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{0.02{t}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & =0.02{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{0.02} \\\\ {t}_{1} & ≈34.657\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest integer, we conclude that it takes $35$ years for the population to double."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176472 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c84b2630-bd68-5970-b8fb-928cd66d7b9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$27$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174971} {:answer/id #uuid "15fa94fc-08a1-5fae-89a1-488a8da09988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$20$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174972} {:answer/id #uuid "3715cd1f-b09c-5da9-b08e-a923882637fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$22$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174973} {:answer/id #uuid "38add315-3ed6-5faf-96d9-5f874834d91d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$17$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174974} {:answer/id #uuid "3df4f670-b5df-51bd-9036-065925a81f5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$25$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174975}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f41bc711-7b5a-5ba6-bf53-c676249dfe25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174970}] :question/id #uuid "f7ddddb9-8f4a-5d7d-9cef-2d227179074b" :question/math-academy-id "q-339309" :question/problem "The rate at which the number of particles in a radioactive substance decays $N(t)$, after $t$ years, can be modeled by the differential equation\n$\\frac{dN}{dt} =-0.05N$.\nIf there are $150000$ particles today, how many years will it take for the number of particles to decrease to $50000$? Round your answer to the nearest year.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176473 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "030b203c-bf99-57ae-aa05-b07007ad6946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$500$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311985} {:answer/id #uuid "38557608-89fc-5dc0-bbb9-6ebe27987a24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$328$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312430} {:answer/id #uuid "9aa789f7-7802-556b-8eb9-f47f06b5fa9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,000$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313200} {:answer/id #uuid "adc81cce-93fc-5d31-b9f1-14d0b8b9db8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$461$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313333} {:answer/id #uuid "c3dcac77-0fd5-5160-9b26-9093411e477d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$625$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "adc81cce-93fc-5d31-b9f1-14d0b8b9db8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$461$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313333} :answer-field/id #uuid "daddff5b-7eb1-5923-aef8-3e5f6126e74a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314617}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "81552c15-02b2-55ae-8888-41525d5709b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-339321" :question/problem "The rate at which the number of particles in a radioactive substance decays $N(t),$ after $t$ years, can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=-0.01N.\n$$\n\nHow many years will it take for the number of particles to be $1\\%$ of the original number? Round your answer to the nearest year." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We model the number of particles using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=-0.01N.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nN(t)={N}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.01t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${N}_{0}$ is the initial number of particles.\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the number of particles is $1\\%$ of its initial number. That is,\n\n$$\nN({t}_{1})=0.01{N}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.01{N}_{0} & ={N}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.01{t}_{1}} \\\\ 0.01 & ={e}^{-0.01{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.01) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-0.01{t}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.01) & =-0.01{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.01)}{0.01} \\\\ {t}_{1} & ≈460.517\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest integer, we conclude that it takes $461$ years for the number of particles to be $1\\%$ of the original number."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314756 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02bee4f3-b655-5e19-8244-51de7d7873c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.1$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311981} {:answer/id #uuid "825af481-1e71-5353-9701-9b79d446e326" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.2$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313025} {:answer/id #uuid "98974d5d-404e-5347-bde0-987fd09ec5ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.8$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313184} {:answer/id #uuid "dbded73a-35b2-5b76-9303-73936db48bbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.4$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313706} {:answer/id #uuid "e5672fee-4675-514e-b601-1ada102f0be1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.5$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98974d5d-404e-5347-bde0-987fd09ec5ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.8$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313184} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbdadf62-38ec-57ce-bd56-7f4c297a1eb3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b71a4a6b-7596-5bb0-a385-748571c7e195" :question/math-academy-id "q-339219" :question/problem "The price $P(t)$ of a particular good varies according to the differential equation ${P}^{′}(t)=rP(t),$ where $t$ is the time, measured in years, and $r>0.$ It is known that the price of the good tripled after the first six years. How long did it take for the price to double from the initial price?" :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)={P}_{0}{e}^{rt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{0}$ is the price of the good when $t=0.$\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the price equals $2{P}_{0}.$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({t}_{1}) & =2{P}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{P}_{0}{e}^{r{t}_{1}}=2{P}_{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{0}{e}^{r{t}_{1}} & =2{P}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{P}_{0}}{e}^{r{t}_{1}} & =2\\cancel{{P}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{r{t}_{1}} & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{r{t}_{1}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ r{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{r}\\,\\,(∗)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate $r.$ We're told that the price of the good tripled after the first six years. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(6)=3{P}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{P}_{0}{e}^{6r}=3{P}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for $r,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{0}{e}^{6r} & =3{P}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{P}_{0}}{e}^{6r} & =3\\cancel{{P}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{6r} & =3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{6r}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ r & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this into $(∗)$ and evaluating, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{6}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & ≈3.8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314757 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e61fea9-46c4-571c-af25-0b4b8d2233e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.6$ months" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312339} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc1ff51-5a3a-5093-854b-97d69e9689ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.4$ months" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312354} {:answer/id #uuid "36d8246d-2859-53c5-bd0c-93a6a1e60033" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.5$ months" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312416} {:answer/id #uuid "40eba7b4-5d78-5d84-810b-a485aa1dfd2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.8$ months" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312495} {:answer/id #uuid "b8a4eb57-5d98-50c6-a832-625ce8d73b3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.7$ months" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313410}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e61fea9-46c4-571c-af25-0b4b8d2233e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.6$ months" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312339} :answer-field/id #uuid "2892f9f8-27d6-5f43-8b29-32d833188429" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314118}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "30f450e1-9f50-50d0-a5b8-9d9411257241" :question/math-academy-id "q-339223" :question/problem "The population $P(t)$ of mice in a forest varies according to the differential equation ${P}^{′}(t)=rP(t),$ where $t$ is the time, measured in months from the start of the observation period, and $r>0.$  It is known that the mouse population quintuples (increases fivefold) after the first two months. How long does it take for the mouse population to increase eightfold from the start of the observation period? Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)={P}_{0}{e}^{rt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{0}$ is the mouse population when $t=0.$\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the mouse population equals $8{P}_{0}.$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({t}_{1}) & =8{P}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{P}_{0}{e}^{r{t}_{1}}=8{P}_{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{0}{e}^{r{t}_{1}} & =8{P}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{P}_{0}}{e}^{r{t}_{1}} & =8\\cancel{{P}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{r{t}_{1}} & =8 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{r{t}_{1}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ r{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡8 \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}{r}\\,\\,(∗)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate $r.$ We're told that the population quintuples after the first two months. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(2)=5{P}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{P}_{0}{e}^{2r}=5{P}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for $r,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{0}{e}^{2r} & =5{P}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{P}_{0}}{e}^{2r} & =5\\cancel{{P}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{2r} & =5 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2r}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ r & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this into $(∗)$ and evaluating, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}{r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5} \\\\  & ≈2.6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314758 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f80ff36-5f4b-50b2-82c2-4245585a8511" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7.7$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312852} {:answer/id #uuid "72d8a0ef-a660-50d1-8a17-b313b5e65157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7.6$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312882} {:answer/id #uuid "8f244e3a-fd2e-548e-b768-a48abde793be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7.4$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313117} {:answer/id #uuid "8fc5130c-2075-5be4-bdb6-de43f8dfa703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7.3$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313122} {:answer/id #uuid "f83f564a-cdd1-5464-a4c4-4004788eb34b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7.5$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72d8a0ef-a660-50d1-8a17-b313b5e65157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7.6$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312882} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c9c2d1b-c0ae-5a85-b5e5-e995c225e2bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f3648d5b-2812-593c-b336-3a42fac9c75a" :question/math-academy-id "q-339228" :question/problem "A car is traveling along a straight road to a particular destination. The remaining distance $D(t)$ varies according to the differential equation ${D}^{′}(t)=-rD(t),$ where $t$ is the time, measured in hours, and $r>0.$ Two hours into the journey, the remaining distance is three-quarters of the journey's total distance. How long does it take for the remaining distance to reduce to one-third of the journey's total distance? Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nD(t)={D}_{0}{e}^{-rt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${D}_{0}$ is the distance when $t=0.$\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the distance equals $\\frac{1}{3}{D}_{0}.$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D({t}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{3}{D}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{D}_{0}{e}^{-r{t}_{1}}=\\frac{1}{3}{D}_{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{0}{e}^{-r{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{3}{D}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{D}_{0}}{e}^{-r{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cancel{{D}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{-r{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-r{t}_{1}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\\\ -r{t}_{1} & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{r}\\,\\,(∗)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate $r.$ We're told that two hours into the journey, the remaining distance is three-quarters of the journey's total distance. Therefore,\n\n$$\nD(2)=\\frac{3}{4}{D}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{D}_{0}{e}^{-2r}=\\frac{3}{4}{D}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for $r,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{0}{e}^{-2r} & =\\frac{3}{4}{D}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{D}_{0}}{e}^{-2r} & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cancel{{D}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{-2r} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-2r}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right) \\\\ -2r & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.75) \\\\ r & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.75)}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this into $(∗)$ and evaluating, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.75)}{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.75)} \\\\  & ≈7.6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314759 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a0520c3-e283-58f3-baff-9d5ff2eaafc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "224\\,605" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312807} {:answer/id #uuid "92a536e0-705e-5c9f-a739-2e3bf45137cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "422\\,645" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313153} {:answer/id #uuid "a11fbf09-7344-5596-8d3c-6acaae712209" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "242\\,650" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313241} {:answer/id #uuid "a24a6dbe-3af2-5058-9dd2-3dfcc115b242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "422\\,564" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313252} {:answer/id #uuid "ebf1db16-173f-5e97-9402-aff3a357c03b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "242\\,506" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a0520c3-e283-58f3-baff-9d5ff2eaafc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "224\\,605" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312807} :answer-field/id #uuid "347db3bb-bdcc-55cd-b9cb-b997b7fef1e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "61e72b24-d234-5774-a24e-6716abb34347" :question/math-academy-id "q-339235" :question/problem "The number of subscribers $S(t)$ to a new video streaming service after $t$ weeks can be modeled by the differential equation ${S}^{′}(t)=0.11S(t).$ If the streaming service had $60\\,000$ subscribers after $20$ weeks, calculate the number of subscribers after $32$ weeks." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nS(t)={S}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.11t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{0}$ is the initial number of subscribers.\n\nWe are given that after $20$ weeks, $60\\,000$ people subscribed to the service. So, substituting $t=20$ and $S(20)=60\\,000$ into our general solution and solving for ${S}_{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(20) & ={S}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.11\\cdot 20} \\\\ 60\\,000 & ={S}_{0}\\,{e}^{2.2} \\\\ {S}_{0} & =\\frac{60\\,000}{{e}^{2.2}} \\\\  & ≈6\\,648.190,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nHence, our particular solution is\n\n$$\nS(t)=6\\,648.190\\,{e}^{0.11t}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the number of subscribers after $32$ weeks. So, substituting $t=32$ into our particular solution, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(32) & =6\\,648.190\\,{e}^{0.11\\cdot 32} \\\\  & =6\\,648.190\\,{e}^{3.52} \\\\  & ≈224\\,605,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest whole number. \n\nTherefore, the streaming service will have about $224\\,605$ subscribers after $32$ weeks."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314760 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32e89fd4-e58a-593b-8f00-f33b319a7909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,986" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312387} {:answer/id #uuid "44435a2a-89d3-5df0-a9b6-a9646359b754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,869" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312524} {:answer/id #uuid "5c979f1c-9d22-55c9-9621-dd73bf835582" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,986" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312713} {:answer/id #uuid "63712074-5c21-51f3-ab48-9ccbc7787b41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,896" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312765} {:answer/id #uuid "6c57f052-c720-5948-b0f3-cd9058de6ef1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,698" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c57f052-c720-5948-b0f3-cd9058de6ef1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,698" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312823} :answer-field/id #uuid "5654e905-e68b-5bb6-b2c8-0ebfd04f25e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dde71f8f-bba2-5206-a37a-f8c5e12abf05" :question/math-academy-id "q-339237" :question/problem "The population $P(t)$ of rabbits in a forest after $t$ weeks can be modeled by the differential equation ${P}^{′}(t)=0.11P(t).$ If there were $786$ rabbits after $8$ weeks, calculate the rabbit population after $15$ weeks." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.11t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{0}$ is the initial number of rabbits.\n\nWe are given that after $8$ weeks, there were $786$ rabbits. So, substituting $t=8$ and $P(8)=786$ into our general solution and solving for ${P}_{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(8) & ={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.11\\cdot 8} \\\\ 786 & ={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.88} \\\\ {P}_{0} & =\\frac{786}{{e}^{0.88}} \\\\  & ≈326.019,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nHence, our particular solution is\n\n$$\nP(t)=326.019\\,{e}^{0.11t}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the number of rabbits after $15$ weeks. So, substituting $t=15$ into our particular solution, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(15) & =326.019\\,{e}^{0.11\\cdot 15} \\\\  & =326.019\\,{e}^{1.65} \\\\  & ≈1\\,698,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest whole number. \n\nTherefore, there will be about $1\\,698$ rabbits after $15$ weeks."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314761 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a94e178-872a-5394-bd64-e96b34719501" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\$67\\,644$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312169} {:answer/id #uuid "40173d1a-be28-5d46-89ba-d34fb366cb51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\$67\\,464$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312492} {:answer/id #uuid "7d807833-cdc4-5215-b04e-29d78ae6e0c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\$74\\,744$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312981} {:answer/id #uuid "cc71998b-6c27-5808-914a-78b4d089ef12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\$64\\,677$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313574} {:answer/id #uuid "fb90e615-d0a4-5654-9c2b-f25c1e73af2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\$77\\,644$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb90e615-d0a4-5654-9c2b-f25c1e73af2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\$77\\,644$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313983} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b16b7c9-2d85-5039-859e-9db8c3f309cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314079}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "433e9fa5-4fc4-562a-b02f-bef18204002b" :question/math-academy-id "q-339238" :question/problem "The value $P(t)$ of a particular luxury car $t$ years after the year $2010$ can be modeled by the differential equation ${P}^{′}(t)=0.025P(t).$ If the car's value in $2016$ was $\\$62\\,000,$ calculate its value in the year $2025.$" :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.025t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{0}$ is the initial value of the car.\n\nWe are given that the value in $2016$ was $\\$62\\,000.$ So, substituting $t=6$ and $P(6)=$62\\,000$ into our general solution and solving for ${P}_{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(6) & ={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.025\\cdot 6} \\\\ 62\\,000 & ={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.15} \\\\ {P}_{0} & =\\frac{62\\,000}{{e}^{0.15}} \\\\  & ≈53\\,363.895,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nHence, our particular solution is\n\n$$\nP(t)=53\\,363.895\\,{e}^{0.025t}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the car's value $15$ years after $2010.$ So, substituting $t=15$ into our particular solution, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(15) & =53\\,363.895\\,{e}^{0.025\\cdot 15} \\\\  & =53\\,363.895\\,{e}^{0.375} \\\\  & ≈77\\,644,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest whole number. \n\nTherefore, the car's value in $2025$ will be $$77\\,644.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314762 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a7edd03-fa67-5d21-beed-89a0ad93bf7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,547" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312054} {:answer/id #uuid "14ad8962-fa18-5d97-92e2-64f32b048dbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\,542" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312118} {:answer/id #uuid "98c13441-efbd-50fe-a81c-83cb16087acb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,247" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313186} {:answer/id #uuid "c789770a-5f75-55c1-8782-00ed437140d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17\\,542" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313535} {:answer/id #uuid "d5b5c280-2b89-5865-bddf-f714da3cbe1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\,257" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a7edd03-fa67-5d21-beed-89a0ad93bf7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,547" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312054} :answer-field/id #uuid "a70d82d1-60c4-582d-9aaa-1dea259c92d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c1343f64-d830-54e8-a05f-16c3f85806cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-339239" :question/problem "The population $P(t)$ of fish in a pond after $t$ weeks can be modeled by the differential equation ${P}^{′}(t)=0.09P(t).$ If the fish population was $8\\,000$ after $18$ weeks, calculate the fish population after $23$ weeks." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.09t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{0}$ is the initial fish population.\n\nWe are given that after $18$ weeks, the population was $8\\,000.$ So, substituting $t=18$ and $P(18)=8\\,000$ into our general solution and solving for ${P}_{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(18) & ={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.09\\cdot 18} \\\\ 8\\,000 & ={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{1.62} \\\\ {P}_{0} & =\\frac{8\\,000}{{e}^{1.62}} \\\\  & ≈1\\,583.190,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nHence, our particular solution is\n\n$$\nP(t)=1\\,583.190\\,{e}^{0.09t}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the fish population after $23$ weeks. So, substituting $t=23$ into our particular solution, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(23) & =1\\,583.190\\,{e}^{0.09\\cdot 23} \\\\  & =1\\,583.190\\,{e}^{2.07} \\\\  & ≈12\\,547,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest whole number. \n\nTherefore, approximately $12\\,547$ fish will be in the pond after $23$ weeks."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314971 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0565c9a1-3267-5c8e-9342-8d4792792693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "47" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312010} {:answer/id #uuid "30337cd4-b663-516c-812c-f06d183d5cf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312364} {:answer/id #uuid "94928d5f-97ee-5b8d-89ac-d044f0019c17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313167} {:answer/id #uuid "af9c85d6-087e-5c2a-b134-ecf511b1d930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313344} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d77545-249a-5fbd-a02a-be4c5c92046d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "41" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30337cd4-b663-516c-812c-f06d183d5cf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312364} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d647f9e-721a-5afb-a7d1-b8fcf37b997b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3cd19394-53ef-5fb8-bd17-0eb0faf26bf7" :question/math-academy-id "q-340560" :question/problem "The rate at which the price of a particular product changes $P(t),$ after $t$ years, is given by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=0.042P.\n$$\n\nIf the price today is $\\$120,$ how many years will it take for the price to increase to $\\$750?$\nRound your answer to the nearest year." :question/worked-solution "We model the price growth using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=0.042P.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.042t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that the price today is $\\$120,$ so we have ${P}_{0}=120.$\n\nTherefore, the price $P$ of the product after $t$ years is\n\n$$\nP(t)=120\\,{e}^{0.042t}\\,.\n$$\n\nTo find how many years it takes for the price to grow to $\\$750,$ we substitute $P(t)=750$ into the equation above and solve for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \n\\begin{aligned}750 & =120\\,{e}^{0.042t} \\\\ \\frac{750}{120} & ={e}^{0.042t} \\\\ {e}^{0.042t} & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{0.042t}) & =ln\\,\\left(\\frac{25}{4}\\right) \\\\ 0.042t & =ln\\,\\left(\\frac{25}{4}\\right) \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{0.042}ln\\,\\left(\\frac{25}{4}\\right) \\\\ t & ≈43.633\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest integer, we conclude that it takes $44$ years for the price to increase to $\\$750.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314974 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fc8944a-13a5-55e1-baea-2473938c18a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312355} {:answer/id #uuid "590814cd-c9fe-5330-b591-7744b2b0994d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312687} {:answer/id #uuid "af8362cb-505d-5c5e-bca8-3b99748b910d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313343} {:answer/id #uuid "db5caf0d-a47b-59ae-aea3-3c15e9e96f2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313699} {:answer/id #uuid "dcce7f65-8fec-5193-9c5d-1c0ebe7d046c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313713}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fc8944a-13a5-55e1-baea-2473938c18a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312355} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa94f990-23f5-5687-aa38-4fb4ca551042" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9a3b77e3-9271-505a-b532-ff83bec5b611" :question/math-academy-id "q-340589" :question/problem "The rate at which the population of yeast cells in a laboratory culture grows $Y(t),$ after $t$ days, can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}Y}{\\text{d}t}=0.55Y.\n$$\n\nHow many days does it take for the population to triple? Round your answer to the nearest day." :question/worked-solution "We model the population growth using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}Y}{\\text{d}t}=0.55Y.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nY(t)={Y}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.55t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Y}_{0}$ is the initial population.\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the population is triple the initial number. That is,\n\n$$\nY({t}_{1})=3{Y}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y({t}_{1}) & ={Y}_{0}{e}^{0.55{t}_{1}} \\\\ 3{Y}_{0} & ={Y}_{0}{e}^{0.55{t}_{1}} \\\\ 3 & ={e}^{0.55{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{0.55{t}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3 & =0.55{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{0.55} \\\\ {t}_{1} & ≈1.997\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest integer, we conclude that it takes $2$ days for the population to triple."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314975 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61d810b5-ac9b-5cf5-a917-0955e40c9408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312754} {:answer/id #uuid "749159b2-00f8-5d78-8646-66fe68e7e8f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312903} {:answer/id #uuid "79149f9b-a309-57bf-b8f7-110044933ef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312942} {:answer/id #uuid "bc774898-673b-52ae-9af3-e1f42aa48699" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313444} {:answer/id #uuid "e823f19f-d050-5a40-af7d-cfa06cccd7fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79149f9b-a309-57bf-b8f7-110044933ef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312942} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2448028-6127-5455-83dc-555dd9134367" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314634}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2adfa444-40b8-5d0c-9139-cf04f4466b88" :question/math-academy-id "q-340590" :question/problem "The rate at which the number of independent mobile applications on a digital marketplace grows $A(t),$ after $t$ years, can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=0.35A.\n$$\n\nHow many years does it take for the number of applications to triple? Round your answer to the nearest year." :question/worked-solution "We model the growth in the number of applications using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=0.35A.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nA(t)={A}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.35t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${A}_{0}$ is the initial number of applications.\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the number of applications is triple the initial number. That is,\n\n$$\nA({t}_{1})=3{A}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A({t}_{1}) & ={A}_{0}{e}^{0.35{t}_{1}} \\\\ 3{A}_{0} & ={A}_{0}{e}^{0.35{t}_{1}} \\\\ 3 & ={e}^{0.35{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{0.35{t}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3 & =0.35{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{0.35} \\\\ {t}_{1} & ≈3.139\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest integer, we conclude that it takes $3$ years for the number of applications to triple."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314984 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12c97067-bc1a-5bb4-9b21-b80fb19902ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.1$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312108} {:answer/id #uuid "4db430f2-654c-5aa5-9aca-2bcdacf9c579" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.3$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312605} {:answer/id #uuid "5036298a-4dca-5bfa-a7cd-e3ef5c0c8ca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4.2$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312624} {:answer/id #uuid "5044d680-27a4-517a-89ce-26786a755309" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7.7$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312625} {:answer/id #uuid "c34fe4ec-2d79-59f8-a576-858cc2044119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.5$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5036298a-4dca-5bfa-a7cd-e3ef5c0c8ca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4.2$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312624} :answer-field/id #uuid "69f24794-2d25-50e0-aeaa-6503d71f5044" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314306}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6567430c-bf28-52b9-99c5-6d8d25bfbb17" :question/math-academy-id "q-340627" :question/problem "The rate at which the cultivated area of a particular region grows $A(t),$ after $t$ years, can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=0.08A.\n$$\n\nHow many years does it take for the cultivated area to increase by $40\\%?$ Round your answer to the nearest decimal place." :question/worked-solution "We model the growth of the cultivated area using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=0.08A.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nA(t)={A}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.08t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${A}_{0}$ is the initial cultivated area.\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the cultivated area has increased by $40\\%.$ That is,\n\n$$\nA({t}_{1})=1.4{A}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A({t}_{1}) & ={A}_{0}{e}^{0.08\\cdot {t}_{1}} \\\\ 1.4{A}_{0} & ={A}_{0}{e}^{0.08{t}_{1}} \\\\ 1.4 & ={e}^{0.08{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.4) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{0.08{t}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.4) & =0.08{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.4)}{0.08} \\\\ {t}_{1} & ≈4.205\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest decimal place, we conclude that it takes $4.2$ years for the cultivated area to increase by $40\\%.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314998 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ae05134-f6e2-5eb5-83ea-79e2693d30db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$20$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312451} {:answer/id #uuid "7dc16bec-89d0-5dd1-8007-877349e2b516" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$15$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312984} {:answer/id #uuid "87665202-0bd2-594f-ac61-3d092ca81dc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313057} {:answer/id #uuid "87cbcfd1-e4bb-5648-8cde-a686a40e549f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313060} {:answer/id #uuid "8db079a8-dfbe-5c11-a431-6c7df25c23f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87665202-0bd2-594f-ac61-3d092ca81dc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313057} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9176d7c-c76d-503d-9253-5d1155d8246e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314512}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "373ad6da-bb3e-5f1e-bbec-eccfa8b969de" :question/math-academy-id "q-340691" :question/problem "A database is being scanned by cleanup software. The rate at which the number of unprocessed records decays $R(t),$ after $t$ minutes, can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}t}=-0.25R.\n$$\n\nIf there are $20\\,000$ unprocessed records at the start of the scan, how many minutes does it take for the number of unprocessed records to decrease to $1\\,000?$ Round your answer to the nearest minute." :question/worked-solution "We model the number of unprocessed records using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}t}=-0.25R.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nR(t)={R}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.25t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${R}_{0}$ is the initial number of unprocessed records.\n\nWe're told that there are $20\\,000$ unprocessed records at the start of the scan, so we have ${R}_{0}=20\\,000.$\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the number of unprocessed records has decreased to $1\\,000.$ That is,\n\n$$\nR({t}_{1})=1\\,000.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\,000 & =20\\,000{e}^{-0.25{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\frac{1\\,000}{20\\,000} & ={e}^{-0.25{t}_{1}} \\\\ 0.05 & ={e}^{-0.25{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.05) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-0.25{t}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.05) & =-0.25{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.05)}{-0.25} \\\\ {t}_{1} & ≈11.982\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest integer, we conclude that it takes $12$ minutes for the number of unprocessed records to decrease to $1\\,000.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314999 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "074f27fe-48ec-5fd6-b878-e20f44130083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ weeks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312023} {:answer/id #uuid "0dc64377-0f91-5810-a055-cde6a593ab49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$20$ weeks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312074} {:answer/id #uuid "204003f6-d4ab-54e4-bbec-820641979fd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12$ weeks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312219} {:answer/id #uuid "39d3368c-b444-548d-846c-762ec18dbc66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$25$ weeks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312444} {:answer/id #uuid "71b54870-62b8-567f-a882-4050bcc09da9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$17$ weeks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312869}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71b54870-62b8-567f-a882-4050bcc09da9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$17$ weeks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312869} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cdc2e1c-978e-556b-8887-4522ee2aca37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "967293c6-9fbb-56f8-8f71-51865c3e9dc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-340695" :question/problem "The rate at which the number of active users of a mobile app decreases $U(t),$ after $t$ weeks from launch, can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}U}{\\text{d}t}=-0.04U.\n$$\n\nIf there are $40\\,000$ active users at launch, how many weeks does it take for the number of active users to decrease to $20\\,000?$  Round your answer to the nearest week." :question/worked-solution "We model the number of active users using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}U}{\\text{d}t}=-0.04U.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nU(t)={U}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.04t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${U}_{0}$ is the initial number of active users.\n\nWe're told that there are $40\\,000$ active users at launch, so we have ${U}_{0}=40\\,000.$\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the number of active users has decreased to $20\\,000.$ That is,\n\n$$\nU({t}_{1})=20\\,000.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20\\,000 & =40\\,000{e}^{-0.04{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\frac{20\\,000}{40\\,000} & ={e}^{-0.04{t}_{1}} \\\\ 0.5 & ={e}^{-0.04{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.5) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-0.04{t}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.5) & =-0.04{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.5)}{-0.04} \\\\ {t}_{1} & ≈17.329\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest integer, we conclude that it takes $17$ weeks for the number of active users to decrease to $20\\,000.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315026 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43d692b1-5fa3-5443-8e2b-d56d1c6ce751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ seconds" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312520} {:answer/id #uuid "530902c5-d390-5db2-b9fb-fd400aff538e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12$ seconds" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312649} {:answer/id #uuid "8a08f9d7-68a0-520b-8bb0-c03d461d119a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ seconds" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313072} {:answer/id #uuid "9f5ff3bb-28be-51e7-a9d9-9682b706ed52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$15$ seconds" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313229} {:answer/id #uuid "baa0dfcf-5ad4-50f0-a265-2f8ee50a3b1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ seconds" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313430}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a08f9d7-68a0-520b-8bb0-c03d461d119a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ seconds" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313072} :answer-field/id #uuid "d89c7f80-0f93-53d8-867f-6eada62a908a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3cc8d56e-ed47-51c9-97e5-45af8b08915d" :question/math-academy-id "q-340837" :question/problem "A balloon is being deflated. The rate at which the diameter of the balloon decays $D(t),$ after $t$ seconds, can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=-0.20D.\n$$\n\nHow long does it take for the balloon’s diameter to reduce to $15\\%$ of its original diameter?  Round your answer to the nearest second." :question/worked-solution "We model the diameter of the balloon using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=-0.20D.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nD(t)={D}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.20t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${D}_{0}$ is the initial diameter.\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the diameter of the balloon is $15\\%$ of its original diameter. That is,\n\n$$\nD({t}_{1})=0.15{D}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.15{D}_{0} & ={D}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.20{t}_{1}} \\\\ 0.15 & ={e}^{-0.20{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.15) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-0.20{t}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.15) & =-0.20{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.15)}{0.20} \\\\ {t}_{1} & ≈9.486\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest second, we conclude that it takes $9$ seconds for the balloon’s diameter to be $15\\%$ of its original diameter."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315027 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d230ae9-0c96-5b3a-aa43-99105d046b7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ months" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312327} {:answer/id #uuid "3e5376b9-c55b-57e2-8e5f-72423c4dae5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12$ months" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312478} {:answer/id #uuid "8e47ddf0-38b7-5940-837a-12f0ef49aa81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$75$ months" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313110} {:answer/id #uuid "a15b88fa-e103-5cd1-b2af-c85055342c51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$25$ months" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313243} {:answer/id #uuid "ed2b3468-dd26-5a39-8242-f5914c5a3db2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ months" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313870}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e5376b9-c55b-57e2-8e5f-72423c4dae5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12$ months" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312478} :answer-field/id #uuid "392d4793-caac-52d5-9656-460e4c52bab2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d08199dc-60c1-55f4-b810-164242e2f155" :question/math-academy-id "q-340839" :question/problem "The amount of charge stored in a battery is decreasing due to self-discharge. The rate at which the amount of stored charge decays $W(t),$ after $t$ months, can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t}=-0.025W.\n$$\n\nHow long does it take for the amount of stored charge to be $75\\%$ of the original amount? Round your answer to the nearest month." :question/worked-solution "We model the amount of stored charge using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t}=-0.025W.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nW(t)={W}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.025t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${W}_{0}$ is the initial stored charge.\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the amount of stored charge is $75\\%$ of the original amount. That is,\n\n$$\nW({t}_{1})=0.75{W}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.75{W}_{0} & ={W}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.025{t}_{1}} \\\\ 0.75 & ={e}^{-0.025{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.75) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-0.025{t}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.75) & =-0.025{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.75)}{0.025} \\\\ {t}_{1} & ≈11.507\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest month, we conclude that it takes $12$ months for the amount of stored charge to be $75\\%$ of the original amount."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402221 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "386007b3-91ce-5982-8534-d31233322fdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6.3$ weeks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402223} {:answer/id #uuid "0a01f341-43f3-528c-b811-0f8051a23c3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ weeks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402224} {:answer/id #uuid "8ac92552-dc2b-5d29-986a-281a4e64330d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6.4$ weeks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402225} {:answer/id #uuid "c1738319-0cbe-5aa8-beb9-61d8c99fe62b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6.5$ weeks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402226} {:answer/id #uuid "aa2f0725-683a-50fc-855c-66c7d9ba756f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6.2$ weeks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "386007b3-91ce-5982-8534-d31233322fdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6.3$ weeks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402223} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4ae4df5-40ba-5454-aa69-0f62becda821" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b562af2b-8343-5000-806c-d01887adea11" :question/math-academy-id "q-339222" :question/problem "The number of subscribers $N(t)$ to an e-learning platform varies according to the differential equation ${N}^{′}(t)=rN(t),$ where $t$ is the time since the platform was launched, measured in weeks, and $r>0.$ It is known that the number of subscribers doubles after the first four weeks. How long does it take for the number of subscribers to triple after the platform is launched? Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nN(t)={N}_{0}{e}^{rt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${N}_{0}$ is the number of subscribers when $t=0.$\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the number of subscribers equals $3{N}_{0}.$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N({t}_{1}) & =3{N}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{N}_{0}{e}^{r{t}_{1}}=3{N}_{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{N}_{0}{e}^{r{t}_{1}} & =3{N}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{N}_{0}}{e}^{r{t}_{1}} & =3\\cancel{{N}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{r{t}_{1}} & =3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{r{t}_{1}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ r{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{r}\\,\\,(∗)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate $r.$ We're told that the number of subscribers doubles after the first four weeks the platform is launched. Therefore,\n\n$$\nN(4)=2{N}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{N}_{0}{e}^{4r}=2{N}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for $r,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{N}_{0}{e}^{4r} & =2{N}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{N}_{0}}{e}^{4r} & =2\\cancel{{N}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{4r} & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{4r}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ r & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this into $(∗)$ and evaluating, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & ≈6.3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402228 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10efe48b-a9f4-5aa8-8700-8513aef294db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.4$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402230} {:answer/id #uuid "9483f82e-0758-5787-8ce2-48f26c64a23d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402231} {:answer/id #uuid "85698021-f6eb-5674-af9e-26f88bf35ee6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.3$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402232} {:answer/id #uuid "ec0b804f-53f8-5360-9835-166b54051187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.1$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402233} {:answer/id #uuid "85d74bd3-72ef-5c17-a201-f97592fcc512" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.5$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85698021-f6eb-5674-af9e-26f88bf35ee6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.3$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402232} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa46205e-ab90-5810-a697-993aab9f4c54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402229}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8375c335-8cf2-5c82-b2c9-770890e7d65a" :question/math-academy-id "q-339226" :question/problem "The value $V(t)$ of a tech device varies according to the differential equation ${V}^{′}(t)=-rV(t),$ where $t$ is the time, measured in years, and $r>0.$ It is known that the value reduces by one-half after the first year. How long does it take for the value to reduce to one-fifth of its original value? Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nV(t)={V}_{0}{e}^{-rt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${V}_{0}$ is the value when $t=0.$\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the value equals $\\frac{1}{5}{V}_{0}.$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{5}{V}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{V}_{0}{e}^{-r{t}_{1}}=\\frac{1}{5}{V}_{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{0}{e}^{-r{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{5}{V}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{V}_{0}}{e}^{-r{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cancel{{V}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{-r{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-r{t}_{1}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right) \\\\ -r{t}_{1} & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{r}\\,\\,(∗)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate $r.$ We're told that the value reduces by one-half after the first year. Therefore,\n\n$$\nV(1)=\\frac{1}{2}{V}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{V}_{0}{e}^{-r}=\\frac{1}{2}{V}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for $r,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{0}{e}^{-r} & =\\frac{1}{2}{V}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{V}_{0}}{e}^{-r} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cancel{{V}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{-r} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-r}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\ -r & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ r & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this into $(∗)$ and evaluating, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & ≈2.3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402235 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98555ddd-6e37-50dd-95fe-cea932afb900" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\$31.13$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402237} {:answer/id #uuid "bcf3ab14-e63c-5a07-8f00-c6624545c11c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\$116.01$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402238} {:answer/id #uuid "e23ede75-277e-5685-b92b-dcc0d6291e35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\$122.03$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402239} {:answer/id #uuid "145dc5fb-065b-57b6-ab94-00b00d6949c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\$113.25$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402240} {:answer/id #uuid "41ca3324-7989-5875-90e2-784c63f6f17b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\$114.93$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402241}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41ca3324-7989-5875-90e2-784c63f6f17b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\$114.93$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402241} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6cbfe2e-3815-5b36-b83f-63a2f8eb905b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402236}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d1d42a16-3984-5dae-bf8b-539545f414f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-339248" :question/problem "The rate of increase ${E}^{′}(t)$ of the average monthly cost of electricity in a particular region varies according to the differential equation ${E}^{′}(t)=0.02E(t),$ where $t$ is the time, in years, since the year $2017.$ In $2021,$ the average monthly cost was $\\$124.50.$ What was the average monthly cost of electricity in $2017?$" :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nE(t)={E}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.02t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${E}_{0}$ is the average monthly cost of electricity in $2017.$\n\nWe are given that the monthly residential electricity cost was $\\$124.50$ in $2021,$ so after $t=4$ years. Hence, substituting $t=4$ and $E(4)=124.50$ into our general solution and solving for ${E}_{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(4) & ={E}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.02\\cdot 4} \\\\ 124.50 & ={E}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.08} \\\\ {E}_{0} & =\\frac{124.50}{{e}^{0.08}} \\\\  & ≈114.93\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest cent.\n\nTherefore, the average monthly cost in $2017$ was $\\$114.93.$"}]}
